  "metadata": {
    "title": "GamER: Gameplay Physiological Signal Dataset for Emotion Recognition",
    "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.8191877",
    "publication_date": "2023-07-28",
    "description": "<p>A dataset of physiological signals (EEG, ECG, EDA, EOG and Respiration) collected from 48 subjects while they were playing simple games designed to elicit emotional responses.</p>\n\n<p>Data were collected using the Biosignals PLUX Researcher kit, which allows up to 10 hours of signal recordings at up to 3kHz sampling rate and 16-bit resolution per channel, while recording data from up to eight sensors simultaneously.</p>\n\n<p>The dataset consists of 3 files:</p>\n\n<ol>\n\t<li>data.zip: Contains the physiological signals. They are organized in 1 folder per subject, which contains the recorded data in .h5 and .txt format as provided by the Researcher kit.</li>\n\t<li>emotion_annotation.csv: Contains the emotion annotation that was produced by a psychologist based on the subjects self-reports and by examining the video of the data collection process (the videos are not included for privacy reasons)</li>\n\t<li>video_offsets.csv: Additional information for synchronizing the video-based annotations with the signals.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>For more information, refer to the accompanying publication.</p>\n\n<p><em>Disclaimer: All subjects have provided written consent for the publication of their anonymized physiological signals.</em></p>",
